  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal. Find $(2u+3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot (v-w).$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2u+3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot (v-w) & =(2u)\\cdot v+(3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot v-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =2(u\\cdot v)+3(w\\cdot v)+u\\cdot v-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+3(w\\cdot v)+0-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =3(w\\cdot v)-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =3(0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & -(0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =3(0+2+0+-2)-(0-1-9-4) \\\\  & =0-(-14) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
